(ASK YOUR PDFs | A NOTE ON READING RESEARCH PAPERS) Tj
T*
(An original sample document for the Folio demo collection) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Begin with a question) Tj
T*
(Research papers are easier to read when the reader has a purpose. Before reading every section ) Tj
T*
(closely, write down the question you hope the paper will answer. It might concern the task, the ) Tj
T*
(data, the method, the evaluation, or the limits of a result. A clear question helps distinguish ) Tj
T*
(the paper's main contribution from background detail.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Start with the title, abstract, and conclusion. The abstract gives a compressed account of the ) Tj
T*
(problem and result, but it omits details by design. The conclusion often restates the authors' ) Tj
T*
(interpretation and may name limitations or future work. Treat both sections as a map, not as a ) Tj
T*
(substitute for the evidence in the paper.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. Read the problem and method) Tj
T*
(The introduction should explain why the question matters and what gap the authors claim to ) Tj
T*
(address. Identify the stated contributions and note which are empirical claims, which are design ) Tj
T*
(choices, and which are broader interpretations. A claim that a method is ?better? is incomplete ) Tj
T*
(until the comparison, metric, and evaluation setting are clear.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the methods section, look for enough detail to understand what was done. Record the data ) Tj
T*
(source, selection criteria, preprocessing steps, baseline systems, and evaluation measures. Ask ) Tj
T*
(whether the experiment isolates the factor the authors want to study. If data is filtered or ) Tj
T*
(labels are constructed, those decisions can affect what the findings mean.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Figures and tables often hold the most concrete evidence. Read their captions and axes, check ) Tj
T*
(sample sizes, and compare the reported result to the baseline. A large relative improvement can ) Tj
T*
(correspond to a small absolute change. A single aggregate score can hide uneven performance ) Tj
T*
(across groups or conditions. The paper's own explanation should be read alongside the displayed ) Tj
T*
(numbers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. Separate results from interpretation) Tj
T*
(A result is an observation under a particular experimental setup. An interpretation explains ) Tj
T*
(what the authors think that observation means. A causal conclusion needs a design that can ) Tj
T*
(support causal reasoning; correlation alone does not show that one factor produced another. A ) Tj
T*
(benchmark result demonstrates performance on that benchmark, not universal usefulness in every ) Tj
T*
(setting.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Check whether uncertainty is reported. Confidence intervals, repeated runs, or significance ) Tj
T*
(tests may show how stable a result is, although each has assumptions. When a paper compares many ) Tj
T*
(systems or settings, look for details about selection and reporting. A careful reader ) Tj
T*
(distinguishes a promising result from a fully established conclusion.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4. Place limitations in context) Tj
T*
