(Limitations are part of the evidence. They might include a narrow sample, a short study period, ) Tj
T*
(a particular language, a simulated environment, or a benchmark that does not represent ) Tj
T*
(deployment. These constraints do not automatically invalidate the work. They define the ) Tj
T*
(situations to which the finding can reasonably be applied.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Look for risks that follow from the method as well as limitations the authors explicitly name. A ) Tj
T*
(model trained on historical data may reproduce patterns in that data. A usability study with ) Tj
T*
(expert participants may not predict first-time users' experience. A reported average may obscure ) Tj
T*
(different outcomes for specific populations. These questions help a reader judge relevance to a ) Tj
T*
(new problem.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(5. Build a concise reading note) Tj
T*
(A useful note records the research question, the central method, the strongest evidence, and the ) Tj
T*
(most important limitation. Add page references while reading so that later claims can be checked ) Tj
T*
(quickly. Distinguish your own observations from the authors' statements. If you compare several ) Tj
T*
(papers, use the same categories for each to make similarities and differences visible.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A disciplined reading note does not need to retell every section. It should help a future reader ) Tj
T*
(reconstruct why the paper matters and where its conclusions are supported. When a claim cannot ) Tj
T*
(be traced to a figure, method, or cited passage, keep it tentative. Returning to the original ) Tj
T*
(page is a normal part of research, not a failure of memory.) Tj
T*
